      "surface_form": "A curated dataset of 476 binary ionic compound melting temperatures spanning 0-4,000°C was compiled for machine learning model development."
    },
    {
      "id": "clai_00053",
      "type": "claim",
      "surface_form": "The best one-step gradient-boosted tree regression model achieves 165±4 K MAE and 0.89 R² on the test set for melting temperature prediction."
    },
    {
      "id": "clai_00055",
      "type": "claim",
      "surface_form": "Cluster 1 (198 compounds, mostly ionic halides and oxides) achieves 136±5 K MAE and 0.92 R², while Cluster 2 (222 compounds, mostly covalent carbides and nitrides) achieves 189±7 K MAE and 0.90 R²."
    },
    {
      "id": "clai_00057",
      "type": "claim",
      "surface_form": "Models trained without elastic moduli features yield errors greater than 400 K, demonstrating that elastic features are necessary for accurate melting temperature predictions."
    },
    {
      "id": "clai_00058",
      "type": "claim",
      "surface_form": "Very-high melting temperatures (>3150 K) appear only in Cluster 2, predominantly in metal carbides, reflecting the covalent bonding character of these materials."
    },
    {
      "id": "clai_00059",
      "type": "claim",
      "surface_form": "Tree ensemble methods (gradient-boosted trees and random forest regression) substantially outperform linear models, which achieve MAE >240 K and RMSE >340 K, at least 100 K worse than non-linear approaches."
    },
    {
      "id": "clai_00060",
      "type": "claim",
      "surface_form": "The two-step clustering approach provides interpretability by revealing different melting physics dominated by bonding types, with Cluster 1 governed by ionic bonding features and Cluster 2 by covalent bonding features."
    },
    {
      "id": "clai_00062",
      "type": "claim",
      "surface_form": "Cluster 2 melting temperatures can be accurately represented with symbolic models depending on only two features: squared cohesive energy and inverse square root of periodic groups or electronegativities."
    },
    {
      "id": "clai_00295",
      "type": "claim",
      "surface_form": "An MLIP trained on pristine crystalline phases may not be appropriate for studying amorphous phases of the same material."
    },
    {
